      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one thing i'm seeing is that some small icons and specific button states seem to be getting lost or ignored by the screen reader, even though they looks fine visually. it's like they're just... hidden or causing weird little glitches in the navigation flow. how should i approach documenting these kinds of edge case errors in my report?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Document the edge cases by including screen reader narrative output when these elements are focused or activated. Take note of where the screen reader fails to announce the element or provides incorrect information.\n\n1. Capture the browser's and screen reader's version to rule out known bugs.\n2. Record the exact position of the problem within the app, including highlighting code snippets or navigation flows that led to the issue.\n3. Use browser tools to demonstrate the issue visually, such as 'accessibility inspector'. Compare what the user can see on the screen with what the screen reader says.\n4. Summarize the impact on the user's experience, explaining why it’s an obstacle.\n5. Provide steps to reproduce and any workaround temporarily implemented by the developers.\n6. Reference relevant WCAG checkpoints that are not met in these particular scenarios.\n\nGenerate a prioritized list of the most critical issues first, as repetitive errors can interrupt the user experience more significantly. For each issue, reflect on whether it is a technical glitch, a missing element, or a lack of proper semantic annotation and arrange recommendations based on the urgency of resolution.",
